1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Bc4 Qa5 8. O-O O-O 9. Nb3 Qc7 10. f4 d6 11. Be2 a5 12. a4 Nb4 13. Bf3 Be6 14. Rf2 Qc8 15. h3 Nd7 16. Nd4 Bc4 17. Rd2 Rd8 18. Rb1 {White is patiently building up the pressure whilst safeguarding his queenside pawns with his rooks. However, after Black’s next move, White espies the chance to fall upon the almost-deserted black king.} Nc5 19. Bg4 Qc7 20. f5 Nd7 21. fxg6 hxg6 22. Ncb5 Qb8 23. b3 Bxb5 24. axb5 $5 Na2 {The threat was 25.c3, winning the knight, but the text move takes advantage of the fact that White did not recapture on b5 with his knight, which would cover the c3 square. In fact, I believe that Black was falling into an incredibly deep trap.} ({Here Houdini 4 Pro suggests} 24... Nf6 $5 25. Ne6 $5 {and considers the position equal. JS}) 25. Bxd7 $1 Nc3 26. Qg4 $1 Nxb1 ({Had Black foreseen White’s next move, he might have played} 26... Rxd7 {However, I would not be surprised if Wise actually planned to reply to 26...Rxd7 with} 27. Rf1 ({intending} 27. Qxd7 Nxb1 {with a slight edge to White since the knight will take a bit of time returning from b1.}) 27... Qe8 ({or} 27... e6 28. Nxe6 $1 {with a very strong attack}) 28. Nf5 $1 gxf5 {(otherwise the main defender of the king is exchanged)} 29. Rxf5 {and Black has difficulty coping with the threats to his scattered pieces.}) 27. Be6 $3 {An absolutely stunning move. The threat is 28 Qxg6! Rf8 29 Nf5, mating on g7, so Black has no real choice but to accept the sacrifice.} fxe6 28. Qxg6 Rf8 29. Nxe6 Rf7 {... and White had to have seen that his next move breaks down the black defence:} 30. Rf2 $1 Qf8 (30... Qe8 {loses to} 31. Bh6 {, intending 32.Ng5! followed by 33.Qh7+ and 34.Qh8 mate. In reply to Black’s despairing queen sacrifice, White simply ignores it!}) 31. Ng5 $1 b6 32. Qh7# {Source: Obituary of Derek M Wise published in En Passant, 2011, by David Lemoir.} 1-0

